 time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 11, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n5le01ob04re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<p><strong>Peter</strong>: Now I see. What about high-level languages?</p>\n<p><strong>Danny</strong>: When people use the term programming languages, they actually refer to high-level languages. Programmers might want to write programs using high-level languages, such as Java, Fortran, or C++, because they are closer to the logic of human language; you use words and symbols, and you ought to use a specific syntax depending on the language. Additionally, you might want to use a high-level language code to run it on different hardware because it is portable.</p>", "image": "enpe7n5le01ob04re2img02.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "40000", "text": "<p><strong>Danny</strong>: Hi, Peter! Where are you going?</p>\n<p><strong>Peter</strong>: Hi! I&rsquo;m meeting Mr. McGill in an hour. He&rsquo;s going to explain to me what high-level and low-level languages are. Tomorrow, I&rsquo;m giving an oral presentation about it, and I&rsquo;m a bit confused.</p>\n<p><strong>Danny</strong>: Don&rsquo;t worry. Actually, I&rsquo;m taking an exam at the faculty later! I&rsquo;ll walk with you and tell you what I know about it.</p>\n<p><strong>Peter</strong>: That&rsquo;d be great!</p>\n<p><strong>Danny</strong>: Well, as you know, there are two types of programming languages: high-level and low-level languages. Low-level programming languages are machine-oriented as they&rsquo;re used to communicate directly to computer hardware. There are two types of low-level languages: machine language and assembly language. Machine language is absolutely difficult for humans to understand as it is written in binary code; however, assembly language, which uses English-like instructions, makes machine code more readable by programmers. You ought to use a translator program called assembler to translate the assembly language into machine language.</p>", "image": "enpe7n5le01ob04re2img01.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "115000", "text": "<p><strong>Peter</strong>: What if I need to program a piece of hardware? Can I use a high-level language?</p>\n<p><strong>Danny</strong>: Actually, you&rsquo;d better use a compiler or an interpreter. These computer programs translate high-level language to machine code, just that they do it differently.</p>\n<p><strong>Peter</strong>: How&rsquo;s that?</p>\n<p><strong>Danny</strong>: Well, a compiler translates the code before you use the program. You ought to compile the code first before running it. This way, the code will run faster. You might want to use a compiler if you need to run the program repeatedly as the compiler will give you a file with an .exe extension. On the other hand, an interpreter will translate the code line during the execution; it is not going to be as fast as a compiled code, but surely it&rsquo;ll be more flexible and interactive at the moment of translation.</p>\n<p>Peter: That was such a good explanation. I&rsquo;d better call Mr. McGill and tell him I won&rsquo;t need his help. Thanks a lot!</p>", "image": "enpe7n5le01ob04re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le01ob04re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}